"""Original organisational safeguarding teaching and fictional review tasks."""
def build(c, m):
    return c(68, 'Safeguarding Organisations and Safer Programmes', 'Safeguarding\nOrganisations and\nSafer Programmes',
        'Review a fictional organisation’s safeguarding arrangements for responsibilities, safer operations, reporting access and accountable improvement without investigating real allegations.', [
        m('Organisational responsibility and safer roles', 'Identify how a fictional organisation’s responsibilities extend beyond possessing a safeguarding policy.', [
            'Organisations can affect children through staff conduct, activities, operations and partners, including work that does not involve a dedicated children’s service. Safeguarding therefore belongs in ordinary planning and management rather than being treated as a single specialist’s private concern. The KCS standards describe organisational responsibilities across policy, people, procedures and accountability. These are practice comparators, not evidence that a particular organisation is accredited or that harm has been prevented. In this course, use them to ask original questions about responsibility and implementation in a fictional organisation. [S41]',
            'A written commitment needs a clear scope and an authorised owner. Consider who is covered, how people learn about their responsibilities and who can act when a concern involves a person with authority. A policy kept in an office may be unavailable to a volunteer, partner or child affected by the organisation’s work. The learner should identify these practical gaps without copying a source policy or representing the worksheet as a complete compliance assessment. Local law, organisational structure and qualified safeguarding advice determine how actual arrangements should operate. [S41]',
            'Safer roles also require appropriate recruitment, induction, supervision and expectations about conduct. A completed check is one part of a broader process; it cannot guarantee that someone will never cause harm. Likewise, a training attendance list does not establish that staff understand or apply the relevant arrangements. In the invented scenario, ask what the organisation would need to verify before assigning responsibilities and how concerns can be raised about unsuitable conduct. Avoid prescribing a particular national screening process as universally available or sufficient. [S41, S42]',
            'Clear role boundaries protect both children and those working with them. A learner may identify a missing responsibility, but should not appoint themselves as an investigator, statutory decision-maker or clinical provider. The CPMS emphasise staff competence and appropriate support in humanitarian child-protection work. A fictional role description should distinguish routine activity supervision, designated safeguarding responsibilities and matters that need qualified external arrangements. An introductory course or badge cannot substitute for a suitable appointment, verified competence or an organisation’s responsibility to provide support and oversight. [S42, S41]'],
            'A fictional organisation has a policy signed by its founder but gives volunteers no induction, treats one background check as a guarantee of safety and assigns a new learner to investigate staff allegations.',
            'Write an original responsibility review identifying four implementation gaps, the appropriate owner for each and the boundary on the learner’s role.',
            'Separate a written commitment from implementation. Include induction, ongoing supervision, suitable conduct expectations and qualified allegation-response arrangements; do not describe a screening check or course as a guarantee.', ['S41', 'S42'], [
                ('Safeguarding responsibilities extend across organisational operations.', 'A fictional organisation says safeguarding matters only in its children’s club. What should its review also consider?', 'How staff, operations, communications and partners elsewhere may affect children.', ['Only the club’s opening hours.', 'Whether children appear in the organisation’s name.', 'Whether indirect contact automatically removes safeguarding responsibility.'], 'Organisational activities can affect children beyond a dedicated service.'),
                ('A signed policy needs practical implementation.', 'The fictional policy is signed but inaccessible to volunteers. What is missing?', 'Suitable communication, understanding and implementation of responsibilities.', ['A claim that the signature alone proves safe practice.', 'A rule that volunteers need no information.', 'An assumption that the policy has been independently certified.'], 'A commitment must be usable by those expected to act on it.'),
                ('Recruitment checks cannot guarantee future safety.', 'A fictional manager describes one screening check as proof that a worker cannot cause harm. What is more accurate?', 'It is one safeguard within suitable recruitment, conduct, supervision and response arrangements.', ['It removes the need for supervision.', 'It guarantees that no concern will ever arise.', 'It transfers every safeguarding duty to the screening provider.'], 'No single check establishes complete or permanent safety.'),
                ('Training attendance is distinct from demonstrated practice.', 'An invented team records attendance at an induction but checks nothing else. What remains unknown?', 'Whether staff understand and can apply the relevant responsibilities and procedures.', ['Whether any attendance record exists.', 'Whether attendance automatically demonstrates every professional skill.', 'Whether policy review is now permanently unnecessary.'], 'Participation in training does not itself establish competent implementation.'),
                ('A learner’s safeguarding role must remain bounded.', 'A fictional role description appoints a new course learner to investigate an actual allegation independently. What should change?', 'Identify the authorised qualified response arrangements and keep the learner within an appropriate supervised role.', ['Retain the appointment because the learner has read a policy.', 'Ask the learner to obtain public witness accounts for coursework.', 'Treat a badge as an investigation licence.'], 'Learning does not confer investigation or statutory authority.')]),
        m('Safer programme design partnerships and communication', 'Analyse a fictional activity for foreseeable safeguarding risks, responsibilities and conditional controls.', [
            'Programme design can create safeguarding risks even when staff intend to help. Consider the setting, transport, supervision, accessibility, communications and how children may seek help. The task is to identify a plausible pathway to harm and a feasible prevention or response measure, not to produce a claim that risk has disappeared. KCS guidance connects safeguarding with ordinary organisational systems and risk review. An original fictional analysis should name the activity, the concern, the responsible owner and the evidence needed before a proposed control can be treated as operating. [S41]',
            'Partnership does not remove an organisation’s responsibility to consider how its work affects children. Clarify what each partner will do, which standards and local procedures apply, how concerns move between the organisations and what happens if an arrangement fails. A signed agreement may record expectations but does not prove that a partner has the capacity to fulfil them. In the invented scenario, retain uncertainty about an unverified service or untrained supervisor. Do not describe the academy as certifying a partner or supplying a complete contract through a course worksheet. [S41]',
            'Communications require attention to children’s dignity, privacy and safety as well as visibility for a programme. An identifiable photograph, story or location may reveal more than organisers intend. Consent and permission questions require the appropriate local process, child involvement and qualified review; an informal classroom assumption is insufficient. The CPMS communications standard treats risk assessment as relevant before advocacy and publicity. For the learner’s exercise, use invented scenarios and avoid reproducing actual child images, personal stories or case details. A compelling campaign is not automatically a safe campaign. [S42, S41]',
            'Controls need review when circumstances change. An activity may become unsuitable if a venue loses an accessible entrance, supervision is unavailable or the proposed transport arrangement has not been confirmed. An appropriate decision may be to change, postpone or stop the activity through the responsible authority. Record the reason and the unresolved conditions without blaming a child for being unable to participate. This is introductory planning practice, not authorisation to organise real field activities with children. Actual delivery requires suitable local procedures, competence, resources and oversight. [S41, S42]'],
            'An invented programme advertises a children’s event before confirming supervisors or accessible transport. Its partner promises support without a named owner, and a campaign plan includes identifiable child stories.',
            'Prepare a conditional activity note linking each concern to a feasible control, responsible role, verification need and stop-or-change condition. Use no real child information.',
            'Keep delivery conditional on suitable supervision, access and partner arrangements. Treat publicity and identifiable stories as safeguarding questions requiring an appropriate authorised process, not as automatic marketing assets.', ['S41', 'S42'], [
                ('Programme risk review should connect concerns to feasible controls.', 'A fictional event plan says only keep children safe. What makes its planning more useful?', 'Specific concerns, suitable controls, responsible roles and verification needs.', ['A guarantee that no risk exists.', 'A longer slogan without practical responsibilities.', 'A statement that good intentions replace supervision.'], 'A usable review explains what needs to happen and who is responsible.'),
                ('Partner promises require appropriate verification.', 'A fictional partner offers transport without confirming a responsible person or capacity. What status should the plan retain?', 'Unconfirmed, with a named verification owner and conditional alternative.', ['Guaranteed and fully implemented.', 'Independently accredited by the classroom exercise.', 'Safe because the partner signed a general letter.'], 'An agreement or promise is not evidence of operational capacity.'),
                ('Publicity can create safeguarding risks.', 'An invented campaign proposes publishing a child’s identifiable protection story. What should be considered?', 'Dignity, privacy, safety and the appropriate authorised consent and communication process.', ['Only whether the story attracts attention.', 'Whether removing the child’s first name guarantees safety.', 'Whether every campaign automatically serves the child’s best interests.'], 'Communication decisions require safeguarding attention beyond publicity value.'),
                ('Changing conditions may require changing or stopping an activity.', 'An invented event loses its planned supervision shortly before opening. What is appropriate?', 'Use the responsible authority to review, change or postpone delivery until suitable arrangements exist.', ['Proceed because the event was advertised.', 'Assign unprepared children to supervise each other.', 'Record the missing supervision as already provided.'], 'A public promise does not justify delivery under unmet safeguarding conditions.'),
                ('Access barriers should be addressed through organisational planning.', 'A fictional venue becomes inaccessible to one child. What is a sound planning response?', 'Review suitable alternatives and responsibility without blaming the child for the barrier.', ['Exclude the child without examining the arrangement.', 'Declare that attendance at any venue proves inclusion.', 'Require the child to supply a personal trauma account before an adjustment is considered.'], 'Access is a planning responsibility, not a test of whether a child deserves participation.')]),
        m('Reporting access accountability and learning', 'Review fictional reporting and improvement arrangements without conducting an investigation.', [
            'A reporting route should be understandable, accessible and connected to an appropriate response. A complaint box, phone number or policy paragraph alone does not establish that a child knows how to use it, can do so safely or receives suitable follow-up. KCS guidance treats reporting and responding as locally appropriate organisational arrangements. In the fictional review, ask how people learn about the route, who receives a concern and what happens when the usual contact is implicated or unavailable. Do not invent a universal reporting deadline or an unverified local helpline. [S41]',
            'Receiving a concern is distinct from investigating an allegation or deciding its truth. A designated organisation must use appropriate qualified procedures, including safeguards against conflicts of interest. The learner should recognise when the activity has reached that boundary and identify the relevant responsibility. Avoid public questioning, classroom determinations of guilt or promises that the organisation can resolve every concern internally. An appropriate fictional record can acknowledge that a response is needed without collecting a real child’s account or disclosing identifying information to the class. [S41, S42]',
            'Accountability includes examining whether measures work and correcting gaps. A low number of reports might reflect fewer incidents, but it might also reflect inaccessible channels, fear, poor awareness or incomplete recording. Do not interpret the count alone as proof of improved safety. KCS guidance supports monitoring and review of implementation rather than reliance on policy ownership. An original fictional improvement entry should identify the gap, a responsible owner, the proposed action and the evidence that would support a later review. No source assessment checklist or certification claim is needed. [S41]',
            'Learning from problems should preserve both privacy and an accurate history. An organisation can review an aggregate process failure without circulating a detailed personal account. Changes to a reporting route may need communication, staff support and a check that the revised arrangement is usable. The participation review reminds learners that relationships and context affect whether children are heard. Keep unresolved issues visible and distinguish a recommendation from an implemented change. The worksheet is evidence of reasoning, not an independent audit, a guarantee of safety or completion of a professional safeguarding appointment. [R51, S41]'],
            'An invented organisation reports zero concerns after moving its only channel to an app. A complaint about the usual contact has no alternative route, but the annual report declares the system fully safe.',
            'Draft an original accountability note addressing alternative access, conflict of interest, interpretation of report counts, responsible action and a proportionate review measure.',
            'Zero reports do not establish zero harm. Identify usable alternatives and qualified independent responsibility where needed, then propose review evidence without collecting or publishing real allegations.', ['S41', 'S42', 'R51'], [
                ('A reporting channel needs a suitable response arrangement.', 'A fictional organisation installs a complaint box without naming anyone to review it. What is the central gap?', 'A responsible, suitable process for receiving and responding to concerns.', ['The colour of the box alone.', 'A declaration that the box guarantees confidentiality.', 'A claim that installing a box proves compliance.'], 'A channel without responsibility does not establish a functioning response.'),
                ('An implicated contact requires an appropriate alternative route.', 'An invented concern involves the only named safeguarding contact. What should the organisation have considered?', 'A suitable alternative qualified route and safeguards against conflict of interest.', ['A rule that concerns about the contact cannot be raised.', 'A classroom vote on whether the allegation is true.', 'Automatic publication of the full complaint.'], 'An effective process needs arrangements for conflicts and unavailable contacts.'),
                ('Receiving a concern does not authorise investigation by any worker.', 'A fictional volunteer receives an allegation and proposes interviewing witnesses publicly. What is appropriate?', 'Follow the designated qualified response arrangements and avoid independent public investigation.', ['Proceed because a concern has been reported.', 'Require children to resolve the allegation in a group discussion.', 'Treat the volunteer’s interpretation as a final finding.'], 'Receipt, investigation and adjudication are distinct responsibilities.'),
                ('Zero reports cannot alone prove zero harm.', 'The invented app-only channel receives no reports. Which interpretation is defensible?', 'The count is insufficient; access, awareness, trust and recording need review.', ['Every child is safe because the count is zero.', 'The organisation no longer needs reporting arrangements.', 'Any future report must be false because the previous count was zero.'], 'Reporting numbers have multiple possible explanations.'),
                ('Improvement recommendations need observable follow-through.', 'A fictional review proposes an alternative reporting route. What should accompany it?', 'A responsible owner, implementation conditions and proportionate evidence for a later review.', ['A claim that making the proposal completed the improvement.', 'An invented audit signature.', 'A request for real case files from children in the class.'], 'Accountability distinguishes a recommendation from implementation and review.')])
    ])
